00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.416
Here's how you make the request.

00:02:20.416 --> 00:02:23.414
And basically, if&nbsp;anything goes wrong it just throws an exception.

00:02:23.414 --> 00:02:26.969
So, we'll just return true if no exception&nbsp;is thrown.

00:02:26.969 --> 00:02:30.560
And it says: My website is down.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.331
My website is definitely online. So, clearly,&nbsp;I'm doing something wrong.

00:02:36.331 --> 00:02:41.662
So, I printed out the exception this time and I'm getting an&nbsp;http error zero.

00:02:41.662 --> 00:02:46.156
zero is not a valid http error. So, something else is going on.

00:02:46.156 --> 00:02:49.142
Apparently,&nbsp;this is some kind of security thing with browsers

00:02:49.142 --> 00:02:51.562
Because this is the client-side code,

00:02:51.562 --> 00:02:57.955
this is&nbsp;actually translating into having the client go out and do some kind of http request.

00:02:57.955 --> 00:03:01.852
Apparently, a lot&nbsp;of browsers block this functionality for security reasons.

00:03:01.852 --> 00:03:04.866
The solution is to do this request on&nbsp;the server side instead.

00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:07.087
So, let's figure out how to make some server code.

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:09.977
On the left hand side,&nbsp;we can clearly see client code and server code.

00:03:09.977 --> 00:03:13.088
